diagnosis_text:
The IT department’s L2/L3 path is healthy. Multiple hosts in 192.168.1.0/24 can reach other departments and servers over L3, indicating the “department switch” (switch0) and upstream gateway are functioning. Evidence:
- it_lab1 (192.168.1.3) successfully pinged office_pc (192.168.3.2) with 0% loss, avg ~1.64 ms; server LAN pc1 (1.0.0.5) with 0% loss, avg ~1.59 ms; and internet LAN pc3 (128.168.0.3) with 0% loss, avg ~1.46 ms. it_lab1 also reached a peer (it_lab2, 192.168.1.4) on the same subnet with 0% loss, confirming local L2.
- it_hod (192.168.1.2) and it_lab3 likewise reached office_pc with 0% loss and low RTTs.
- Host configs on IT endpoints show correct addressing and default route via 192.168.1.1.
- switch0 (IT access switch) shows both ports up and bridged (br0 with eth0 uplink to router0 and eth1 to lan_it; all interfaces UP, LOWER_UP). No indication of link or forwarding failure.

Mechanism: If switch0 or its uplink were down, IT hosts would fail to reach off-subnet destinations and intra-VLAN peers; instead, cross-VLAN traffic forwards normally with low latency.

Localization: No fault found on switch0, its uplink (link_r0_sw0), or IT hosts tested.

Root cause: No network outage present; the earlier symptom is not reproducible.

Corrective fix: None required on the network. If users still can’t access “shared drives,” investigate endpoint issues (DNS/name resolution, drive mappings/credentials, or host firewalls) rather than the campus network. === END ===